# AdMitra Website Development Rules

## Color System Guidelines

### 1. Always Use CSS Variables
- **NEVER** use hardcoded hex colors (e.g., `#3b82f6`) in HTML or CSS
- **ALWAYS** use the predefined CSS variables from `:root`
- Example: Use `var(--primary-500)` instead of `#3b82f6`

### 2. Color Palette Structure
```css
/* Primary Colors - Subtle Modern Blue */
--primary-50 to --primary-900

/* Secondary Colors - Complementary Cyan */
--secondary-50 to --secondary-900

/* Accent Colors - Warm Coral */
--accent-50 to --accent-900

/* Neutral Colors */
--gray-50 to --gray-900

/* Semantic Colors */
--success-500, --success-600
--warning-500, --warning-600
--error-500, --error-600
```

### 3. Semantic Color Usage
- **Primary**: Main brand colors, CTAs, navigation highlights
- **Secondary**: Supporting elements, subtle backgrounds
- **Accent**: Highlights, special elements, hover states
- **Gray**: Text, borders, backgrounds
- **Success**: Success states, positive feedback (NOT for branding)
- **Warning**: Caution states, pending actions
- **Error**: Error states, destructive actions

### 4. Gradient Guidelines
- Use predefined gradient classes: `from-primary-500`, `to-primary-600`
- For custom gradients, use CSS variables: `linear-gradient(135deg, var(--primary-500) 0%, var(--primary-600) 100%)`
- **NEVER** use hardcoded Tailwind colors like `from-blue-100 to-sky-100`

### 5. Text Color Guidelines
- Primary text: `var(--text-primary)` or `text-gray-900`
- Secondary text: `var(--text-secondary)` or `text-gray-700`
- Tertiary text: `var(--text-tertiary)` or `text-gray-600`
- Muted text: `var(--text-muted)` or `text-gray-500`
- Inverse text: `var(--text-inverse)` or `text-white`

### 6. Background Color Guidelines
- Primary background: `var(--bg-primary)` or `bg-white`
- Secondary background: `var(--bg-secondary)` or `bg-gray-50`
- Tertiary background: `var(--bg-tertiary)` or `bg-gray-100`

### 7. Border Color Guidelines
- Light borders: `var(--border-light)` or `border-gray-200`
- Default borders: `var(--border-default)` or `border-gray-300`
- Dark borders: `var(--border-dark)` or `border-gray-400`

## Component-Specific Rules

### 8. Badge/Pill Components
- Use primary colors for brand-related badges
- Use success colors ONLY for actual success states
- Example: "Now Piloting" badge should use `from-primary-500 to-primary-600`

### 9. Navigation
- Use gray colors for navigation text
- Use primary colors for active/hover states
- Maintain consistent hover effects

### 10. Cards and Containers
- Use subtle backgrounds: `var(--bg-secondary)` or `var(--bg-tertiary)`
- Use light borders: `var(--border-light)`
- Maintain consistent shadow styles using CSS variables

## Development Best Practices

### 11. Before Adding New Colors
1. Check if existing CSS variables can be used
2. If new colors are needed, add them to the `:root` section
3. Create corresponding Tailwind utility classes if needed
4. Update this rules file with new color usage guidelines

### 12. Code Review Checklist
- [ ] No hardcoded hex colors in HTML/CSS
- [ ] All colors use predefined CSS variables
- [ ] Gradient classes use consistent variable-based approach
- [ ] Color choices align with semantic meaning
- [ ] Accessibility contrast ratios are maintained

### 13. Maintenance
- Regularly audit codebase for hardcoded colors
- Update CSS variables when brand colors change
- Ensure all team members follow these guidelines
- Test color changes across all components

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

### 14. Don't Do This:
```css
/* BAD */
background: #3b82f6;
color: #64748b;
border: 1px solid #f3f4f6;
background: linear-gradient(135deg, #3b82f6 0%, #8b5cf6 100%);
```

```html
<!-- BAD -->
<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-100 to-sky-100">
<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-success-500 to-success-600"> <!-- for branding -->
```

### 15. Do This Instead:
```css
/* GOOD */
background: var(--primary-500);
color: var(--text-tertiary);
border: 1px solid var(--border-light);
background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--primary-500) 0%, var(--accent-400) 100%);
```

```html
<!-- GOOD -->
<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-primary-100 to-secondary-100">
<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-primary-500 to-primary-600"> <!-- for branding -->
```

## Emergency Override
If you absolutely must use a hardcoded color for a specific reason:
1. Document the reason in a comment
2. Add a TODO to replace it with a CSS variable
3. Get approval from the team lead
4. Plan to refactor it in the next sprint

---

**Remember**: Consistency in color usage creates a cohesive brand experience and makes maintenance much easier. When in doubt, ask the team or refer to the design system.
